comments.body:
Last week I updated to `1.26.1` in my single test instance and on my clustered test instance. For both the backup hangs again. While first one or two backups may work, afterwards it fails again. With a bit of luck the backup starts working again 1 or 2 times after restart.

I'm not sure what to do. I cannot use Weaviate when this basic stuff is not working.

@participant1 thanks for the updates! Will check a bit the processes again!

@reporter And do you see any messages related to Tombstone Cleanup in your logs?

Interestingly, here we are seeing `tombstone_cleanup_begin` in an affected Weaviate instance, but until now (three hours later) still no message saying `tombstone_cleanup_complete` (for neither of the collections). So something in that area seems to be blocked.
```
{"action":"tombstone_cleanup_begin","class":"Page_v3","level":"info","msg":"class Page_v3: shard s82Zd0tWGes4: starting tombstone cleanup","shard":"s82Zd0tWGes4","time":"2024-07-30T13:15:47Z","tombstones_in_cycle":8721,"tombstones_total":8721}
{"action":"tombstone_cleanup_begin","class":"PageNode_v3","level":"info","msg":"class PageNode_v3: shard ZrFReopFq7jq: starting tombstone cleanup","shard":"ZrFReopFq7jq","time":"2024-07-30T13:16:02Z","tombstones_in_cycle":168283,"tombstones_total":168283}
```

Thanks @participant1 
It's true. It seems to work when setting it manually.

looking at the [getCores](https://github.com/weaviate/weaviate/blob/64cf510427cacb1a5ac444192bd575b38eccd086/adapters/handlers/rest/configure_api.go#L139-L140) function I think there might be the bug.

> LIMIT_RESOURCES: When set to true, Weaviate automatically limits its resource usage. It sets memory usage to 80% of the total memory and uses all but one CPU core. It overrides any GOMEMLIMIT values but respects GOMAXPROCS settings.

```go
func getCores() (int, error) {
	cpuset, err := os.ReadFile("/sys/fs/cgroup/cpuset/cpuset.cpus")
	if err != nil {
		return 0, errors.Wrap(err, "read cpuset")
	}

	cores := strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(cpuset)), ",")
	return len(cores), nil
}
```
In my environment this /sys path returns `0-7` for 8 cores. And that function returns then 1 in any case.
Will open another bug for that.
